---
import DocsLayout from '../../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import DocFigure from '../../../../components/DocFigure.astro';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Ingest & Add Jobs — MHA Manager"
  description="Bring raw capture footage into your project as Capture Data through Live Link Hub, and fill the queue from the one Add Jobs dialog — project assets, audio, or raw footage — or automatically as takes arrive."
  currentSlug="ingest"
>

  <div class="doc-header">
    <h1 class="doc-title">Ingest &amp; Add Jobs</h1>
    <p class="doc-lead">
      Every footage job runs on a <code>UFootageCaptureData</code> asset. Getting raw footage into
      the project as that asset is <em>ingest</em>, and Epic's Live Link Hub — which bundles Capture
      Manager — is the engine that does it. Getting assets into the queue is
      <strong>Add Jobs...</strong>, the single entry point in the MHA Manager toolbar — or the
      auto-queue watcher, which needs no dialog at all.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Live Link Hub</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      <strong>Live Link Hub</strong> is Epic's first-party capture application and the recommended
      input for MHA Manager. It bundles Capture Manager: connect your capture source (Live Link Face
      on iPhone, a stereo head-mounted camera, or an existing take archive), import the takes, and
      ingest them into your Unreal project. Each take lands in the Content Browser as a
      <code>UFootageCaptureData</code> asset under <code>/Game/CaptureManager/Imports/&lt;Actor&gt;/</code>
      — exactly what every job runs on, with no manual asset assembly.
    </p>
    <DocFigure
      src="/assets/docs/mha/llh-capture-manager.webp"
      alt="Live Link Hub — Capture Manager view with takes selected, ingesting into the Unreal project"
      caption="Live Link Hub's Capture Manager ingests takes straight into the project as Capture Data"
    />
    <p class="section-desc">
      Drive it from Live Link Hub's own window, or from the <strong>External Footage</strong> source
      of the Add Jobs dialog below. Either way, turn on <strong>Auto-Queue Ingested Takes</strong>
      and every take Live Link Hub delivers is classified and queued automatically — capture in one
      window, watch jobs appear in the other.
    </p>
    <div class="callout callout-note">
      Live Link Hub is a separate Epic download and depends on the Live Link / Virtual Production
      plugins. See Epic's Live Link Hub and MetaHuman Animator capture documentation for device
      setup and recording — that side of the workflow is Epic's, not this plugin's.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">The three sources</h2>
    <DocFigure
      src="/assets/docs/mha/ingest-three-sources.webp"
      alt="Add Jobs dialog with the Source picker — Project Assets, Audio, External Footage (Live Link Hub) — and the Project Assets tree of FootageCaptureData assets below"
      caption="The Add Jobs dialog: pick a source at the top, tick takes in the tree below"
    />
    <div class="field-list">
      <div class="src-row header">
        <span>Source</span>
        <span>What it queues</span>
      </div>
      <div class="src-row">
        <span><strong>Project Assets</strong></span>
        <span>One job per <code>FootageCaptureData</code> asset already in the project. The default panel.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="src-row">
        <span><strong>Audio</strong></span>
        <span>Audio-driven jobs from project SoundWaves, WAV files on disk, or a Wwise container. Requires UE 5.8.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="src-row">
        <span><strong>External Footage (Live Link Hub)</strong></span>
        <span>Raw take folders on disk, scanned and then ingested by Live Link Hub. Requires the <code>CaptureManagerEditor</code> plugin (UE 5.7+) and an installed Live Link Hub.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="callout callout-note">
      Nothing is queued until you confirm (or double-click a single take). The dialog only collects
      a selection — the batch tab runs the enqueue afterwards, so cancelling leaves the queue untouched.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Project Assets</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      Every <code>FootageCaptureData</code> asset in the project, shown as a tree that mirrors its
      content path — so an <code>Imports/&lt;Actor&gt;/&lt;Take&gt;</code> layout reads as actor
      folders with takes underneath. Search filters the tree, checkboxes pick takes (a folder
      checkbox toggles everything under it), each row is labelled Stereo or Mono, and the footer
      counts what is checked against the total. Double-clicking a take queues just that one.
    </p>
    <p class="section-desc">
      <strong>Show thumbnails</strong> (below the source picker, off by default) adds a first-frame
      preview to each row. It is off because decoding a frame per take is slow on large projects;
      thumbnails are only decoded once the box is ticked.
    </p>
    <p class="section-desc">
      Each checked take becomes one job, typed automatically: path-name tokens classify
      <code>grid</code>/<code>calib</code> as Stereo Calibration and <code>identity</code>/<code>dna</code>
      as Identity Create, while Mono and Stereo come from the asset itself. See
      <a href="/marketplace/mha-manager/docs/naming-conventions">Naming Conventions</a> for the
      patterns and <a href="/marketplace/mha-manager/docs/job-types">Job Types</a> for what each
      one does.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Audio</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      Audio jobs need no footage, no calibration, and no identity — a voice track is the whole
      input. Three sub-sources feed them:
    </p>
    <div class="field-list">
      <div class="src-row header">
        <span>Sub-source</span>
        <span>Behavior</span>
      </div>
      <div class="src-row">
        <span><strong>Project</strong></span>
        <span>Searches a content folder recursively and queues one job per SoundWave asset found. Each job references the asset directly — nothing is copied or re-imported.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="src-row">
        <span><strong>Import</strong></span>
        <span>Searches a root folder on disk recursively and queues one job per WAV file. The WAVs stay where they are; each job keeps only its path and imports the wave beside the Performance at solve time.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="src-row">
        <span><strong>Wwise</strong></span>
        <span>Queues one job per Sound under a Wwise container, resolved live over WAAPI. See <a href="/marketplace/mha-manager/docs/wwise-audio">Wwise Audio</a>.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="section-desc" style="margin-top:14px;">
      Whichever sub-source is active, the preview list below it is the same: every discovered item
      with a checkbox, a check-all toggle, and a checked count. Items already in the queue are
      skipped, so re-running against the same folder adds nothing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">External Footage (Live Link Hub)</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      For footage that is not in the project yet. Point the dialog at a footage root on disk; it
      walks the folder itself — a plain filesystem scan, because Live Link Hub is not running yet —
      and classifies every take it finds. You check the ones to bring in, and the confirmed set is
      handed to Live Link Hub as ingest device requests.
    </p>
    <DocFigure
      src="/assets/docs/mha/ingest-add-jobs-external.webp"
      alt="Add Jobs dialog on the External Footage source: footage root, fallback actor, schema and destination line, and the scanned take tree with Kind / Take / Actor columns"
      caption="External Footage: the scanned tree groups takes by folder, with kind and actor per take"
    />
    <div class="field-list">
      <div class="src-row header">
        <span>Take kind</span>
        <span>What the scan matched</span>
      </div>
      <div class="src-row">
        <span><strong>Stereo</strong></span>
        <span>A folder holding two videos whose base names match the schema's stereo patterns.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="src-row">
        <span><strong>Mono</strong></span>
        <span>A single video file.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="src-row">
        <span><strong>Archive</strong></span>
        <span>A take folder in an existing capture archive layout.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="src-row">
        <span><strong>Audio</strong></span>
        <span>A loose WAV, when <strong>Include .wav audio as audio takes</strong> is on. Scan-only — audio takes are queued directly in the editor and never go through Live Link Hub. A WAV that belongs to a stereo take is always that take's audio track, never a take of its own.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="section-desc" style="margin-top:14px;">
      Takes the engine would silently drop are still listed, greyed out with the reason — so a
      mislaid take shows up as a problem instead of just going missing. The <strong>actor</strong>
      for each take comes from its folder depth (see the schema below); takes that sit too shallow
      to carry an actor segment fall back to the dialog's fallback actor field.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Teaching the scan your layout</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      By default the scan assumes the take's parent folder is the actor and that footage is
      <code>.mp4</code> or <code>.mov</code>. A stage whose footage is laid out differently can ship
      the rules with the footage: drop a file named <code>MHAIngestSchema.json</code> at the scan
      root and the dialog picks it up automatically, reporting which rules it used.
    </p>
    <div class="code-block">
      <pre><code>{`{
  "version": 1,
  "path_template": "{actor}/{session}/{take}",
  "video_extensions": ["mp4", "mov"],
  "stereo_patterns": ["*_L", "*_R"]
}`}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="field-list">
      <div class="schema-row header">
        <span>Key</span>
        <span>Default</span>
        <span>Meaning</span>
      </div>
      <div class="schema-row">
        <span><code>version</code></span>
        <span><code>1</code></span>
        <span>Schema version. Anything other than 1 is refused and the built-in defaults are used, with a warning in the dialog.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="schema-row">
        <span><code>path_template</code></span>
        <span><code>{`{actor}/{take}`}</code></span>
        <span>Matched from the <strong>end</strong>: <code>{`{take}`}</code> is the last segment and only <code>{`{actor}`}</code>'s distance from it counts. So <code>{`{actor}/{take}`}</code> means "the take's parent folder", at any depth, and an Actor/Session/Take stage ships <code>{`{actor}/{session}/{take}`}</code>. A template with no <code>{`{actor}`}</code> token sends every take to the fallback actor.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="schema-row">
        <span><code>video_extensions</code></span>
        <span><code>["mp4", "mov"]</code></span>
        <span>Which files count as footage. A leading dot is optional and case does not matter.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="schema-row">
        <span><code>stereo_patterns</code></span>
        <span>(empty)</span>
        <span>Wildcards the two video base names in a folder must match for it to count as a stereo take. Empty accepts any two-video folder — the same rule the engine's own auto-discovery uses.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="callout callout-tip">
      Only the keys you include are overridden; everything else keeps its default. An unparseable
      file falls back to the built-in defaults rather than failing the scan, and says so.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Auto-queue on ingest</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      As takes arrive from Live Link Hub / Capture Manager, the <strong>Ingest</strong> section
      of <a href="/marketplace/mha-manager/docs/global-settings">Global Settings</a> can watch the
      project and queue them the moment they appear in the Content Browser — no dialog needed:
    </p>
    <DocFigure
      src="/assets/docs/mha/ingest-autoqueue-settings.webp"
      alt="Global Settings, Ingest section: Auto-Queue Ingested Takes, Auto-Start Batch on Ingest, and Watch Folder"
      caption="The Ingest section of Global Settings"
    />
    <div class="field-list">
      <div class="src-row header">
        <span>Setting</span>
        <span>What it does</span>
      </div>
      <div class="src-row">
        <span><strong>Auto-Queue Ingested Takes</strong></span>
        <span>Watches the project for newly created Footage Capture Data assets and adds each to the queue automatically. Takes already in the queue are not re-added.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="src-row">
        <span><strong>Auto-Start Batch on Ingest</strong></span>
        <span>Starts the batch when auto-queued takes arrive and no batch is running. Takes arriving mid-batch are queued and started on the next idle trigger.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="src-row">
        <span><strong>Watch Folder (optional)</strong></span>
        <span>A content-path prefix to limit the watch, e.g. <code>/Game/CaptureManager/Imports</code>. Empty watches everywhere under <code>/Game</code>.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">What happens to a queued take</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      However a take enters the queue, it is classified automatically and readied to process:
    </p>
    <ul class="bullet-list">
      <li>Its <a href="/marketplace/mha-manager/docs/job-types">job type</a> is derived from the take — path-name tokens drive <code>grid</code>/<code>calib</code> -&gt; Stereo Calibration and <code>identity</code>/<code>dna</code> -&gt; Identity Create, while mono / stereo / audio come from the asset itself.</li>
      <li>Mono takes get a first-frame framing probe that auto-enables body tracking when the whole body is in shot.</li>
      <li>Dependent assets (calibration, identity) are auto-discovered where the folder layout allows, so a stereo take lands ready or clearly marked for what it still needs.</li>
    </ul>
    <DocFigure
      src="/assets/docs/mha/ingest-autoqueue-result.webp"
      alt="Job Queue after auto-queue: a MetaHumanGrid take typed Calibration and Done, two DNAPose takes typed Identity - one Failed, one blocked on Poor Calibration"
      caption="Auto-queued stereo takes, typed by name: the grid take ran as Calibration, the DNAPose takes as Identity"
    />
    <div class="callout callout-tip">
      Keep <code>grid</code>, <code>calib</code>, <code>identity</code>, and <code>dna</code> out of
      folder and take names that are <em>not</em> those take types — those tokens drive
      classification, and a misnamed performance take will be queued as the wrong job. See
      <a href="/marketplace/mha-manager/docs/naming-conventions">Naming Conventions</a>. Once jobs
      are in the queue, <a href="/marketplace/mha-manager/docs/job-options">Per-Job Options</a>
      covers what you can change per take before starting the batch.
    </div>
  </section>

</DocsLayout>

<style>
  .doc-header { margin-bottom: 40px; }

  .doc-title {
    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 4vw, 2.4rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }

  .doc-lead {
    font-size: 1rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    max-width: 620px;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .doc-lead strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 600; }

  .doc-section { margin-bottom: 48px; }

  .section-title {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    margin-bottom: 14px;
    padding-bottom: 10px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .section-desc {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin: 0 0 12px;
  }

  .section-desc strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 500; }

  .field-list {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .src-row, .schema-row {
    display: grid;
    gap: 16px;
    padding: 11px 16px;
    font-size: 0.825rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    align-items: start;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .src-row { grid-template-columns: 1fr 2.4fr; }
  .schema-row { grid-template-columns: 0.9fr 0.9fr 2.6fr; }

  .src-row:last-child, .schema-row:last-child { border-bottom: none; }

  .src-row.header, .schema-row.header {
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  }

  .src-row strong, .schema-row strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 500; }

  code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--accent);
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    padding: 1px 5px;
    border-radius: 3px;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .code-block {
    background: #0a0a0a;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 20px 24px;
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }

  .code-block pre { margin: 0; }

  .code-block code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: #c9d1d9;
    line-height: 1.75;
    white-space: pre;
    background: none;
    border: none;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .callout {
    padding: 14px 18px;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin-top: 16px;
    border-left: 3px solid;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .callout-tip { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 8%, transparent); border-color: var(--accent); }
  .callout-note { background: var(--bg-surface); border-color: var(--border); }
  .callout strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 600; }
  .callout em { color: var(--text); font-style: italic; }

  .bullet-list {
    padding-left: 20px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 8px;
    margin: 0 0 12px;
  }

  .bullet-list li {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.6;
    padding-left: 4px;
  }

  a { color: var(--accent); text-decoration: none; }
  a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .src-row, .schema-row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 4px; }
  }
</style>
